1. **GAP — the general canonical letter is not well-typed from the stated carrier.**

> “At a general level \(i\), fix exact-height normalizers \(\widehat n_i(m)\) … and stage data
> \[
> e_{i+1},\qquad f_{i+1},\qquad u_{i+1}.
> \]”

followed by

> “\[
> z_{i+1}
> =
> \operatorname{res}\!\left(
> \frac{\Phi_i^{e_{i+1}}}{\pi_{i+1}^{u_{i+1}}}
> \right).
> \]”

and, in the proof,

> “Both normalizers have height \(u_{i+1}\), so the quotient has value zero.”

Only \(\widehat n_i(u_{i+1})\) was declared to have that height. No general-level condition on \(\pi_{i+1}\), such as \(dv_i(\pi_{i+1})=1\), was stated. The explicit level-two Bézout formula supplies this at that level but not at arbitrary depth.

Counter-configuration: at some \(i\ge2\), take \(u_{i+1}=1\), an exact-height-one \(\widehat n_i(1)\), and a nonzero \(\pi_{i+1}\) of height \(2\). Then \(\widehat n_i(1)/\pi_{i+1}\) has height \(-1\), so \(\rho_i\notin K_i^\times\); likewise the displayed definition of \(z_{i+1}\) need not be a degree-zero residue. Neither r1 nor r2 excludes this configuration.

2. **GAP — the C5 residue embedding lacks the compatibility imposed later for C6.**

> “For every tower point \(\xi\) carrying the specified residue embedding \(\iota_\xi\),
> \[
> \operatorname{res}\!\left(
> \frac{A(\xi)}{n(k)(\xi)}
> \right)
> =
> \iota_\xi\bigl(\gamma_k(A)\bigr).
> \]”

The proof silently uses

\[
\operatorname{res}\!\left(\frac{\xi^{e_1}}{\pi^h}\right)
=\iota_\xi(\eta),
\]

but C5 never states it. R2-3 adds precisely such a compatibility condition for \(\theta\) in C6, leaving C5 asymmetric and still underdetermined.

Counter-configuration: let \(e_1=h=1\), \(K_1=\mathbf F_4=\mathbf F_2(\eta)\), and choose \(\xi\) with
\[
\operatorname{res}(\xi/\pi)=\eta.
\]
Take the declared embedding to be Frobenius, \(\iota_\xi(\eta)=\eta^2\), which the written C5 hypotheses do not forbid. For \(A(x)=x\), one has \(k=1\), \(n(k)=\pi\), and \(\gamma_k(A)=\eta\). Thus the left side is \(\eta\), while the asserted right side is \(\eta^2\).

3. **GAP — the honesty ledger asserts an untyped character-torsor theorem.**

> “Gauge uniqueness is not asserted. Fixed-coboundary gauges form a torsor under the available character group.”

No gauge space, coefficient action, character group, or closure of the admissible gauges under character multiplication is defined. Equality of coboundaries generally makes the ratio a \(1\)-cocycle; it is an ordinary character only under additional hypotheses such as a trivial coefficient action.

Counter-configuration: let \(G=C_2\) act on \(A=C_3\) by inversion. Every element of \(A\) defines a crossed \(1\)-cocycle, while the ordinary character group \(\operatorname{Hom}(C_2,C_3)\) is trivial. A fixed-coboundary fiber therefore cannot be a torsor under that character group. This does not affect the no-uniqueness fence, but the positive torsor assertion is unsupported at the stated generality.

4. **MINOR — C5 omits the nonzero-polynomial domain needed to define \(k\).**

> “Let
> \[
> A(x)=\sum_{j<D'}a_jx^j,
> \qquad
> k=dv(A),
> \qquad
> i=i(k).
> \]”

Counter-configuration: take every \(a_j=0\). Then \(A=0\) and \(dv(A)=+\infty\) under the usual convention, so \(i(k)\), \(q(k)\), \(n(k)\), and the integer exponents in \(\gamma_k(A)\) are undefined. The subsequent formulas quantify over this case because \(A\ne0\) is never stated.

VERDICT: FINDINGS: 0C + 3G + 1m
